[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mThird fork: the module format for the shared core.[0m You chose one core serving both surfaces, and the two surfaces
  currently load code in incompatible ways — [38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m is a classic script, [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is CommonJS. The core has
  to be loadable by both.

  - [1mDual-format wrapper[0m — [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m detects its environment and either attaches to [38;5;153mglobalThis[39m or sets [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m.
    Buys: nothing else in the repo changes, and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m still opens straight off disk. Costs: ~4 lines of boilerplate
    that reads slightly dated.
  - [1mConvert everything to ES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in package.json, [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m in all
    three existing files. Buys: one clean idiom, no boilerplate, the direction the ecosystem went. Costs: it's a
    refactor of files the logging task didn't otherwise need to touch, and [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m is fetched under CORS rules —
    [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[39m stops working when opened as a [38;5;153mfile://[39m URL[0m and needs a local HTTP server from then on.

  I recommend the [1mdual-format wrapper[0m. Your ask is logging; converting the module system of every existing file is a
  separate change riding along, and it would silently break opening the page from disk. If you're already serving this
  over HTTP and want the ESM cleanup, that's a fine call — I'd just rather you make it deliberately than inherit it from
  a logging ticket.
